We classify the surviving pairs. Suppose $w\circ0+
u=u\circ\lambda$ with $\ell(w)=k$ and $
u\in\operatorname{Wt}\Pi_\lambda$. Since $w\circ0=who-ho$, this reads $
u=u(\lambda+ho)-who$; applying $u^{-1}$ and putting $v=u^{-1}w$ gives $u^{-1}
u=\lambda+ho-vho=\lambda+\sum_{\alpha\in\Pi_v}\alpha$ by [F6]. Now $u^{-1}
u\in\operatorname{Wt}\Pi_\lambda$ because the weight multiset is $W$-stable, so $\lambda-u^{-1}
u\in Q^+$ by [F4]; on the other hand $\lambda-u^{-1}
u=-\sum_{\alpha\in\Pi_v}\alpha$ lies in $-Q^+$. A nonnegative integral combination of positive roots lying in $-Q^+$ is zero, so $\sum_{\alpha\in\Pi_v}\alpha=0$, which forces $\Pi_v=\emptyset$ and hence $v=1$ by [F6]; thus $u=w$ and $
u=w\lambda$.

For the multiplicity of the head, write $J$ for the sum of all proper submodules of $M(w\circ\lambda)$, the unique maximal submodule, so $M/J\cong L(w\circ\lambda)$ is simple by [F5]. The highest weight vector $v$ of $M(w\circ\lambda)$ spans the one-dimensional weight space $M(w\circ\lambda)_{w\circ\lambda}$ and generates the module, so $v
otin J$ and hence $J_{w\circ\lambda}=0$. Refine the filtration $0\subset J\subset M(w\circ\lambda)$ to a composition series; its top factor is $M/J\cong L(w\circ\lambda)$, and any further factor isomorphic to $L(w\circ\lambda)$ would be a subquotient $X/Y$ of $J$ with $(X/Y)_{w\circ\lambda}
e0$, hence would force $X_{w\circ\lambda}
e0$ and so $J_{w\circ\lambda}
e0$, a contradiction. Therefore $[M(w\circ\lambda):L(w\circ\lambda)]=1$.
